ents  in akron  looking at a number of different  rotations  over a long-term experiment all phases  present in all years  in in each of these experiments so  go ahead and so  i went into this just um wanting to  assess  uh the impact that more diverse  rotations would have  on specifically corn yields across  varying degrees of stress and  just as a starting point i wasn't  focused  on a particular type of stress drought  stress  or or too much precipitation  but just looking at kind of a generic  metric of stress  and um set out with just some very  overly simplified hypotheses  um uh admittedly uh uh  just to kind of get a sense of this so  for instance of course  as um stress is increasing on the x-axis  here we expect yields to go down  and the question was to what extent  did coin yields and the diverse  rotations differ in their response to  trap  stress relative to corn yields in  simplified rotations  we could imagine lots of different  scenarios here one  being for instance that actually at low  levels of stress  that's where we see the benefits of  rotation but then those decline as  stress increases  or we could have another type of  response where  when in productive growing conditions  yields are similar especially if there's  sufficient inputs  but then we see the benefits of rotation  appear  when stress increases and so  i set out to test this with a few  different methods i'm just going to show  you  one of those here and you can go on to  the next slide in  so we um used uh some  bayesian multi-level models to test this  as well as uh the  result that i'm showing here which is a  probability analysis uh something that  we  borrowed from the way that  uh insurance actually assesses risks  um looking at uh the probability  uh of crop failure um that is yields  less than the 10 percentile or the  increased opportunity for for so-called  bumper crop crops or crop yields greater  than the 90th percentile  and then comparing those um  probabilities  bet